  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nSunday-Monday:\nAhead of the frontal passage, temperatures remain hot in the\nlower to middle 90s on Sunday afternoon. Meanwhile, moisture\nwill be on increase with dewpoints reaching into the middle to\nupper 60s. The environment by Sunday afternoon will feature\nample instability (MUCAPE 3000-4000 J/kg) overlaying mediocre\nshear (25-30 knots). This would suggest single cells to\nmulticell clusters being the favored storm mode, with a low\nchance (5%) for an isolated strong to severe thunderstorm. This\nis captured in the SPC Marginal (1 of 5) Severe Outlook on\nSunday. Primary hazards would be quarter sized hail and damaging\nwind gusts up to 60 mph. However, most activity is expected to\nremain sub-severe at this time. From a coverage standpoint,\nshowers and thunderstorms are expected to remain scattered in\nnature. The highest coverage looks to occur along and north of\nInterstate 44 on Sunday evening into Sunday night, before the\nfront pushes through into Monday morning. The associated rain\nchances shift east on Monday with the frontal passage. Rainfall\namounts look to remain limited to a quarter to a half inch north\nof Interstate 44, before tapering off to a tenth of an inch into\nsouthern MO. It is worth noting that CAMs have backed off a bit\nwith coverage through Sunday night, which may support lower PoPs\nin future forecast updates if the trend continues. Nonetheless,\nthe limited rainfall amounts will do little to improve worsening\ndrought conditions.\n\nOn Monday, north-northwest winds will gradually usher in a\ncooler airmass after a hot September thus far. Highs will only\ntop out in the upper 70s to lower 80s across the north.\n\nTuesday-Friday:\nBehind the frontal passage, much cooler temperatures overspread\nthe area. Tuesday will feature highs in the 70s, which is near\nto below normal for mid September. A slight trend up into late\nweek, with highs reaching into upper 70s and lower 80s. This\npattern change will be welcoming to the area after a few weeks\nof hot weather. However, there is no mentionable PoPs beyond\nMonday. This period of dry weather will support worsening\ndrought conditions, and a potential early onset of fall fire\nweather season.",
